# Bounded Lite load, report storage failure and recovery

Run this only on a Linux Docker qualification host with enough unused RAM and
disk. It creates new private Compose projects, volumes and an internal network;
it publishes no ports, configures no hardware, sends no external mail and never
mounts an existing deployment volume. Use a reviewed public source export and
locally built candidate image IDs. Do not point the work directory at a live
installation. The driver refuses existing work directories and does not pull.

Extend the command in `docker/INSTALL-ACCEPTANCE` with:

    --report-disk-pressure --soak-seconds 1800

The supported soak range is 60..1800 seconds; 0 disables load. CI uses 60 seconds
as a regression gate. A 30-minute local rehearsal is not a multi-day soak or an
enterprise capacity guarantee. An incomplete or failing run is not acceptance.

## What it measures

- Actual Caddy HTTPS, verified private CA, authenticated Secure cookies and CSRF.
  Normal API rate limits remain enabled.
- Four paced read clients (dashboard, sites, report catalogue and readiness),
  each up to one request per 1.5 seconds. Aggregate read latency and errors are
  reported. This is an explicit offered workload, not maximum sustainable RPS.
- Alternating PDF/CSV generation every ten seconds against eight paused synthetic
  policies, 20,000 incidents (with JSON/text) and 80,000 compliance samples. Each
  report must include the expected counts and return a valid downloadable file.
- 250 synthetic Timescale event rows inserted directly every five seconds.
  This exercises concurrent database writes, NOT adapter/collector ingestion
  throughput. The exact row count must survive encrypted recovery.
- One harmless token-cleanup task on each of the five worker queues every ten
  seconds. Completion latency is measured; fixture task results are forgotten.
- API cgroup memory samples (including the in-container load generator), final
  container OOM/restart state, generated archive hashes, recreation and encrypted
  backup/restore. The load generator consumes part of the API memory budget.

## Deliberate storage failure

The driver temporarily directs only the fixture API report writer to a separate
16 MiB tmpfs, owned by the non-root application user. The probe independently
verifies the mount type and size before filling it to real ENOSPC. It must not
fill the VM root disk, PostgreSQL volume, persistent report archive or any
customer filesystem. Existing report files remain on their original volume.

Both PDF and CSV requests must fail, with no extra catalogue row or partial
render. Database readiness must continue to pass. Once the filler is removed,
both formats must generate and download again. Only the two newly generated
temporary report records are removed before that temporary filesystem goes
away. The driver restores the ordinary report path, recreates the API and
verifies that the original persistent archives still match their hashes.

## Evidence and limits

Only `report.json` is intended for sharing. The private work directory also
contains generated credentials, bearer tokens, encryption test keys, database
dumps and raw logs. Never upload the whole directory. Retain failed receipts;
do not erase a failure by rerunning in the same work directory.

The original Lite acceptance continues after load: recreate application
containers, check every report hash, fence writers, encrypt both database
backups, retain application volumes, stop the source, restore to fresh volumes
and rotate the authentication signing key while preserving vault keys. All
generated report bytes and the event count are checked again on the target.

These fixtures do not establish a fleet size, production RTO/RPO, a safe
customer queue replay policy, multi-host failover, real SMTP delivery, a
production database-full recovery procedure or a report-history deletion
policy. Report and delivery history remain retained; choose and test an explicit
customer retention policy before offering an operational storage guarantee.
